git-notes-memory: a Claude Code plugin that keeps project memory in git notes
instead of CLAUDE.md, so the memory lives in the repo's own object store and
travels with it.

Components
- plugin/scripts/: plain bash + git + jq. lib.sh holds the shared note logic
  (read/write/migrate/merge). There is one script per hook or command, and each
  one exits 0 silently outside a git repo.
- plugin/hooks/hooks.json wires the lifecycle: SessionStart (session-start.sh:
  sync, fetch origin's notes, inject the architecture and learnings notes),
  PreToolUse on Read/Edit/Write/Bash (pre-tool.sh: show file notes),
  PostToolUse on edits (post-tool.sh: carry the note to the new blob, marked
  needs-review), and Stop (stop.sh: remind about unreviewed notes and unrecorded
  learnings).
- plugin/skills/: git-notes (the protocol, not user-invocable), note,
  notes-sync, init. These are thin wrappers that call the scripts.
- Refs: architecture and learnings sit on the root commit, file-notes on blob
  hashes, and meta (per-clone, never pushed) holds last-synced.
- The repo root is the marketplace (.claude-plugin/marketplace.json ->
  ./plugin), so there is no copy to keep in sync. tests/run.sh drives the hook
  scripts against scratch repos using fake hook JSON.

Key decisions
- Notes on blobs are pinned to content, so they are migrated on edit and on
  sync, and found through a `git log --follow` fallback, rather than attached to
  paths.
- Origin's notes are only staged under refs/notes/origin/* until that remote is
  trusted, so an untrusted remote can't inject text into Claude's context.
- Skills are used instead of commands/, because the docs mark commands/ as
  legacy.
